ntColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(499,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> are the eccentricities of an ellipse and a hyperbola, respectively <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.405ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 4599.2 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"7D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-7D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(777.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1833.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2222.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2765.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3210.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"221E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-221E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4210.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<p>Then <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.489ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.697ex\" height=\"2.375ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 5612.1 1049.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FC\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D6FC\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(673,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1298.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(2299,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FD\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D6FD\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(656.4,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3581.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(4581.3,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D6FE\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D6FE\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(627.3,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is equal to:</p>\n","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>18</p>\n"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>22</p>\n"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>26</p>\n"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>34</p>"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>For the quadratic equation</p>\n<p>$$ x^2-ax+2=0 $$</p>\n<p>let its two distinct roots be $e_1$ and $e_2$.</p>\n<p>We are given that these roots are to be interpreted as eccentricities.</p>\n<p>Recall:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>For a hyperbola, eccentricity $e>1$</p></li>\n<li><p>For an ellipse, eccentricity satisfies $0<e<1$</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also, since $e_1,e_2$ are roots of the quadratic, by Vieta's formulas,</p>\n<p>$$ e_1+e_2=a,\\qquad e_1e_2=2 $$</p>\n<p>and since the roots are distinct real roots,</p>\n<p>$$ a^2-8>0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad |a|>2\\sqrt2 $$</p>\n<p>Now we analyse the two sets.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Both $e_1$ and $e_2$ are eccentricities of hyperbolas</h2>\n<p>This means both roots must satisfy</p>\n<p>$$ e_1>1,\\qquad e_2>1 $$</p>\n<p>Since</p>\n<p>$$ e_1e_2=2>0 $$</p>\n<p>the two roots have the same sign.  </p>\n<p>If both are hyperbola eccentricities, both must be positive and greater than $1$.</p>\n<p>Now, for both roots of</p>\n<p>$$ x^2-ax+2=0 $$</p>\n<p>to be greater than $1$, we use the NCERT-style condition:</p>\n<p>For both roots to be greater than $1$, we need</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>roots real and distinct:</p>\n<p>$$    a^2-8>0    $$</p></li>\n<li><p>sum positive:</p>\n<p>$$    a>0    $$</p></li>\n<li><p>value of polynomial at $x=1$ positive, because $1$ must lie to the left of both roots:</p>\n<p>$$    f(1)=1-a+2=3-a>0 \\text{ or } <0?    $$</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Let us be careful.</p>\n<p>Since the parabola opens upward, if both roots are greater than $1$, then $x=1$ lies outside the roots on the left side, so</p>\n<p>$$ f(1)>0 $$</p>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<p>$$ 3-a>0 \\Rightarrow a<3 $$</p>\n<p>Also, for both roots to be greater than $1$, the axis of symmetry should be to the right of $1$:</p>\n<p>$$ \\frac a2>1 \\Rightarrow a>2 $$</p>\n<p>Combining:</p>\n<p>$$ 2<a<3 $$</p>\n<p>But also we need distinct real roots, and in this interval automatically $a>2\\sqrt2$ is needed. Hence</p>\n<p>$$ 2\\sqrt2<a<3 $$</p>\n<p>So,</p>\n<p>$$ (\\alpha,\\beta)=(2\\sqrt2,\\,3) $$</p>\n<p>Thus,</p>\n<p>$$ \\alpha=2\\sqrt2,\\qquad \\beta=3 $$</p>\n\n<h2>2. $e_1$ is eccentricity of an ellipse and $e_2$ is eccentricity of a hyperbola, respectively</h2>\n<p>This means one root lies in $(0,1)$ and the other lies in $(1,\\infty)$.</p>\n<p>Since</p>\n<p>$$ e_1e_2=2>0 $$</p>\n<p>both roots are positive. So the only possibility is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>one root in $(0,1)$</p></li>\n<li><p>the other root greater than $1$</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now let the roots be distinct and positive. Since their product is $2$, if one root is less than $1$, the other must automatically be greater than $2$, hence greater than $1$.</p>\n<p>So we only need one root to be less than $1$.</p>\n<p>For that, $x=1$ must lie between the two roots. Since the parabola opens upward, this happens when</p>\n<p>$$ f(1)<0 $$</p>\n<p>That is,</p>\n<p>$$ 1-a+2<0 $$</p>\n<p>$$ 3-a<0 $$</p>\n<p>$$ a>3 $$</p>\n<p>Also for real distinct roots, $a^2-8>0$, which is already true for $a>3$.</p>\n<p>Hence the set is</p>\n<p>$$ (\\gamma,\\infty)=(3,\\infty) $$</p>\n<p>So,</p>\n<p>$$ \\gamma=3 $$</p>\n\n<h2>3. Find $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$</h2>\n<p>Now,</p>\n<p>$$ \\alpha^2=(2\\sqrt2)^2=8 $$</p>\n<p>$$ \\beta^2=3^2=9 $$</p>\n<p>$$ \\gamma^2=3^2=9 $$</p>\n<p>Therefore,</p>\n<p>$$ \\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=8+9+9=26 $$</p>\n<p>So the correct answer is</p>\n<p>$$ \\boxed{26} $$</p>\n<p>Hence, <strong>Option C</strong>.</p></a></p></a></p>"},"hi":{"direction":null,"comprehension":null,"content":"<p>माना समीकरण <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.584ex\" height=\"2.072ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 6888 915.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(605,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1230.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2231,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2760,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D465\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D465\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" tran